mcp-service-sdk Design Alignment Comparison
Purpose: Below table compares the use of mcp-service-sdk against having each service implement FastMCP directly. It highlights how the shared SDK preserves the architectural guarantees defined in the QSR design and reduces platform risk.
Requirement from Design Doc	With mcp-service-sdk	Without It (Each Service Uses FastMCP Directly)	Delta / Risk if Not Used
Uniform contract across all services	Guaranteed: one consistent describe / read / act shape for every service	Each team defines its own shape	Contract drift across many services; agent needs per-service handling
Agent cannot tell simulated from real	Same to_mcp() contract for real and simulated services	Only works if every team manually matches the same contract	High risk of divergence; breaks a core design rule
Durable, replayable record	Built-in SQLiteLog / JSONLFileLog, replayable and idempotent on ref_id	Each team builds its own store	Extra code per service; likely inconsistency or omission
Foundation for later benchmarking	Telemetry spans and replayable log included	Each team adds its own timing	No comparable metrics across services
Autonomous, event-driven behavior	emit() with log-first flow, delivery fan-out, and subscribe support	Each team wires event emit and delivery separately	More code per service; retry and ordering bugs become likely
Common place for service reporting	Standard event envelope and delivery sink	Divergent event formats	Agent must parse multiple formats
Actuator actions across domains	act_tool and PolicyGate are built in and mandatory	Safety mechanisms are optional per team	Risk of ungated LLM actions against real store systems
Safe autonomous acting	GateLevel supports AUTOMATIC, NOTIFY, NEEDS_APPROVAL, BLOCKED, plus rate limits	Custom or missing safety implementation	Inconsistent safety model and audit gaps
Swap real pipelines in with no agent change	Contract stability enforced through describe	Depends on every team preserving the exact same shape	Replacing simulated with real services may break the agent
Phased rollout across many services	New service mainly adds tools and domain logic	Each new service rebuilds the scaffolding	Hours versus days for onboarding each service
Multi-service actions	Same contract makes cross-service chaining reliable	Cross-service behavior depends on ad hoc implementations	Fragile orchestration across services
Operator visibility into agent behavior	Uniform audit log and action results	Divergent logs and outputs	Weak and inconsistent audit trail
Platform-wide policy change	One library change plus version bump	Separate updates across every service	High coordination cost and missed updates
Security review	Review the shared library once	Review every service individually	Repeated effort across all services
CVE or bug fix	Fix once in the shared library and bump the dependency	Fix independently in every service	Repeated fixes and uneven rollout
 
Explanation
·	mcp-service-sdk is not just a convenience library. It is the mechanism that enforces a uniform MCP contract across services so the agent can interact with every domain in the same way.
·	The SDK centralizes durable logging, replayability, delivery fan-out, telemetry, and action gating. That reduces duplicated implementation effort and prevents each service from inventing its own contract, safety controls, or audit model.
·	This becomes more important as the platform scales. When more services are onboarded, or when a policy or security fix is needed, a shared SDK lets the change be implemented once and then consumed consistently across services.

Recommendation
Recommended approach: Use mcp-service-sdk as the shared foundation for any service that needs to act as an MCP server, and have consuming services depend on it as a version-pinned Git package or tagged release.


